🚀 Day 6 of My DevOps Troubleshooting Series

🔥 Real-Time Kubernetes Scenario: Pods Are Running, But Application Returns 503

Imagine this situation:

Your application is deployed on Kubernetes.

Suddenly, users report:

❌ 503 Service Unavailable

You check Kubernetes and see:

✅ Pods are `Running`

So, what could be wrong?

One important lesson in Kubernetes is:

Running ≠ Ready

A Pod can be running while still being unable to serve application traffic.

🔍 How would I troubleshoot it?

**1️⃣ Check Pod status**

```bash
kubectl get pods -n <namespace>
```

I would check:

• READY status
• Pod restarts
• Pod status

If the Pod shows `0/1`, I would investigate the readiness probe.

2️⃣ Check Pod events

```bash
kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>
```

Look for:

• Readiness probe failures
• Connection refused
• Timeout errors
• HTTP 503 responses

3️⃣ Check application logs

```bash
kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace>
```

I would look for:

• Database connection issues
• Application exceptions
• Configuration problems
• Missing environment variables
• Dependency failures

4️⃣ Check the Kubernetes Service
```bash
kubectl get svc -n <namespace>
kubectl describe svc <service-name> -n <namespace>
```

Then check the endpoints:

```bash
kubectl get endpoints <service-name> -n <namespace>
```

If there are no endpoints, the Service has no healthy Pods to send traffic to.

5️⃣ Verify Service selectors

For example:

```yaml
selector:
 app: backend
```

The Pod labels should match:

```yaml
labels:
 app: backend
```

A selector mismatch can prevent the Service from routing traffic correctly.

6️⃣ Test connectivity from inside the cluster

```bash
kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -n <namespace> -- \
curl http://<service-name>:<port>
```

This helps determine whether the issue is with the application, Service, or external traffic path.

7️⃣ Check Ingress / Load Balancer

If Pods and Services look healthy, I would check:

• Ingress configuration
• Backend Service and ports
• DNS
• Load Balancer health
• Network/security rules

 🎯 Possible Root Cause

For example:

```text
Pods → Running
Pods Ready → 0/1
Service → Exists
Endpoints → None
```

And the readiness probe shows:

```text
Readiness probe failed
HTTP probe returned 503
`

In this case, the application process is running, but Kubernetes does not consider the Pod healthy enough to receive traffic.

I would then investigate the application's health endpoint and logs to identify the actual root cause.

🧠 Key Takeaway

When troubleshooting Kubernetes, don't just check whether Pods are Running

Follow the traffic path:

User → Load Balancer → Ingress → Service → Endpoints → Pod → Application → Dependencies
